NOT A COVERAGE FAILURE, AND THE COVERAGE GATE IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED.
`_uncatalogued_external_modules` counts only a dst that NAMES a module; ARM 1's
edge is the bare placeholder `python:external:0-0:sendall:external_symbol`,
which names none. That skip is correct -- the placeholder is the largest edge
population in a Python repo and identifies no library to report, so counting it
sends nearly every repo to `inconclusive` while telling the reader nothing,
which is the outcome PR #251 already rejected. `complete` still returns True
over it and `test_untyped_receiver_population_is_the_disclosed_residual` still
asserts that; its docstring now says WHY a reader tempted by this item must not
reverse it.

WHAT CHANGED IS THE VERDICT. A fourth caveat kind, `untyped_receiver`, names the
call sites where a method the catalogue declares FOR THE BOUNDARY UNDER CLAIM is
reached through a receiver the analysis could not type. `confirmed` ->
`confirmed_with_caveats`, rc 0 -> rc 3, fail-closed on the INV-pojib precedent.
It is the sibling argument one step over: CAVEAT_OPAQUE_BOUNDARY's own note
draws the auditor distinction between a DISCLAIMER ("I am blind here") and a
QUALIFIED OPINION ("I examined every call; three hand control to git"). "I saw
this call, I know `sendall` is a catalogued net_send method, and I could not
