BOUNDARY SCOPING IS WHAT MAKES IT SHIPPABLE, and the objection it answers was
measured rather than argued away. A DOWNGRADE on this signal was recorded DO NOT
BUILD IT on 2026-08-11: on poetry every boundary would have gone inconclusive
(db_read 234, ipc_recv 160, fs_read 93, ...), because catalogued method names
include close/get/read/write/send. Two things answer it -- the verdict is
QUALIFIED rather than WITHHELD, and a name matches only primitives catalogued
for the CLAIMED boundary, so that measurement's own example (an unrelated dict
`.get`) cannot touch a net_send verdict. The other half of that refutation,
"anti-correlated with the truth", does NOT survive and is not cited: it argued
from `session.post`, absent from the catalogue entirely (INV-fotav's third-party
gap), not reached through an untyped receiver. `sendall` IS catalogued.

THE NOISE OBJECTION SURVIVES IN A WEAKER FORM AND IS MEASURED, NOT WAVED OFF.
Latent population, scoped: poetry 1,099 sites / 9 boundaries; sqlalchemy 6,851 /
10. By NAME it is mostly true rather than noisy -- poetry's 306 fs_read sites are
16 names (read 95, exists 86, open 34, read_text 16) for a package manager that
really does that. The genuine noise is a name catalogued under TWO boundaries:
the same 103 `get()` sites appear under db_read and ipc_recv. An untyped
receiver cannot disambiguate those BY CONSTRUCTION. So the caveat over-reports,
which is the safe direction for a disclosure whose failure mode is certifying
absence -- and an import-based filter that would cut it sharply ("a pathlib.Path
receiver is impossible if the repo never imports pathlib") is REFUSED here in
writing, because a receiver can arrive from a library the repo never imports and
the filter would restore silent confirms for exactly this shape.

The prose is bounded instead: <=5 sites are spelled out as checkable locations;
above that it names the DISTINCT METHODS with a count, because five arbitrary
line numbers out of three hundred is not actionable and "group, read_text,
exists" is. `entries` keeps every site.

