*** THE ITEM STAYS VIOLATED, AND A DISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT SAYS WHY. An
untyped receiver whose callee the catalogue declares NOTHING for still returns
plain `confirmed`:

    def upload(session, url, payload):            -> NET-SEND confirmed
        return session.post(url, data=payload)

    def upload(session: requests.Session, ...):   -> NET-SEND inconclusive,
        return session.post(url, data=payload)       naming requests.Session

with the fs_read control violated in every arm. So this is NOT the third-party
catalogue gap wearing a disguise (INV-fotav): typing the receiver hands the case
to the existing uncatalogued-module gate, which handles it correctly. Receiver
typing is the binding constraint, which puts the case on THIS item's axis and
inside its statement ("including calls into third-party modules, whose absence
from the catalogue makes them unexamined rather than examined-and-clean").

The caveat structurally cannot reach it: `post` is declared for no boundary, so
nothing distinguishes `session.post(...)` from `items.append(1)`, and caveating
every such call is a caveat on essentially every method call in a Python repo --
PR #251's blanket outcome in new clothes. Closing this half needs the receiver
TYPED (INV-linub L3), sequenced behind precision by 0001's <50% band. Pinned by
TestTheResidualThisDoesNotClose, following this item's own precedent: PR #251
also shipped a partial fix and left the item violated with the residual named.

